  "doc.desc": "Diversification removes part of the risk; only what is left gets paid. Starting from the risk floor of N assets, this essay derives the efficient frontier, the capital market line and the Sharpe ratio once a risk-free asset is added, then shows why measured Sharpe ratios and “optimal” portfolios are so unreliable. It then leaves the one-period world: volatility drag, why leveraged ETFs shrink over time, the small extra return from rebalancing, and the Kelly criterion, which says how much to bet and why nobody bets the full amount. Nine hands-on models; mechanisms only, not investment advice.",
